Q1. Why do we use Docker Volumes?

Interview Ready Answer

Docker Volumes provide persistent storage for container data. They store
data outside the container's writable layer, ensuring that the data
remains available even if the container is removed or recreated.

Definition

Docker Volume
- Persistent storage mechanism.
- Stores container data outside the writable layer.

Purpose

• Prevent data loss.
• Share data between containers.
• Store databases, uploaded files, and application data.
• Keep data independent of the container lifecycle.

======================================================================

Q2. What is the difference between a Docker Volume and the Container's Writable Layer?

Interview Ready Answer

A Docker Volume provides persistent storage outside the container's
writable layer. Data stored in a volume remains available even if the
container is removed or recreated.

The container's writable layer is temporary. Any data stored there is
lost when the container is removed.

Definition

Docker Volume
- Persistent storage.
- Independent of container lifecycle.

Writable Layer
- Temporary storage.
- Exists only with the container.

Purpose

Docker Volume

• Long-term data storage.
• Data persistence.
• Data sharing.

Writable Layer

• Temporary runtime changes.
• Logs, temporary files, cache.

ASCII Diagram

Docker Image (Read-only)
        │
        ▼
Container Writable Layer (Temporary)
        │
        │
        └───────────────┐
                        │
Docker Volume           │
(Persistent Storage)    │
        │               │
        ▼               ▼
Container Removed   Writable Layer Deleted
        │
        ▼
Volume Data Still Exists ✅

